From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Benjamin Gregoire <Benjamin.Gregoire@inria.fr>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 17:40:29 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] The safety pass is sound: its assertions imply the safety of
 the program

safety_callP: if the instrumented program sc_prog p runs without failure
in the total mode, the original program runs in the partial mode and
reaches the same final state (wiequiv_f (sm1 := total) (sm2 := partial)
with the eq specification). The proof goes through the expressions
(sc_pexprP: under the generated assertions, what the total mode computes
the partial mode computes too), the left values (sc_lvalsP, where check_xs
is consumed), the assertions and the contracts, then the induction on the
program with the two-mode relational logic. The generated assertion of a
condition implies the condition (sem_cond_interp_safe), so that an
operator, an instruction or an access under its assertions is its total
semantics.

What the proof needs about the operators and the instructions is proved
next to its subject: an operator under its conditions is its total
semantics (sem_sop1_typed_safeE, sem_sop2_typed_safeE, from the
well-formedness of op1_safe and op2_safe and the invariance of a
well-typed condition under the truncation of its arguments,
all_safety_cond_holds_truncate); check_safe_ok; mk_semi_aux_app_sopnE
unfolds the application of an instruction. On the memory, the total read
and write never fail (read_total_ok, write_total_ok), the partial read
succeeds exactly on validr (is_ok_read_partial), and validr implies
validw. syscall_u_toutP (psem_facts.v) describes the unit syscall.

(cherry picked from commit 546ead43009eaf1e69336ee2406303c65b65dd32)
